      "latency_ms": 2640.2,
      "prompt_tokens": 11,
      "completion_tokens": 572,
      "output": "At its most basic level, **water** is a transparent, odorless, and tasteless liquid that is essential for all known life. \n\nTo truly understand what water is, we can look at it through a few different lenses: scientific, ecological, and human.\n\n---\n\n### 1. The Scientific Definition (Chemistry & Physics)\n* **Chemical Formula:** **$\\text{H}_2\\text{O}$** \n  This means every single molecule of water is made up of **two hydrogen atoms** bonded to **one oxygen atom**.\n* **States of Matter:** Water is unique because it is the only natural substance on Earth that exists abundantly in three physical states:\n  * **Liquid** (the water we drink, rivers, oceans)\n  * **Solid** (ice, snow, glaciers)\n  * **Gas** (water vapor, steam, humidity)\n* **The \"Universal Solvent\":** Water is exceptionally good at dissolving other substances (like salt, sugar, and minerals). This property is what allows it to transport vital nutrients and chemicals through living bodies and ecosystems.\n\n### 2. The Planetary Perspective (Earth Science)\n* **Coverage:** Water covers about **71% of the Earth's surface**, mostly in oceans and seas (which hold about 96.5% of all Earth's water).\n* **Scarcity of Fresh Water:** Despite water being everywhere, **only about 2.5% of it is fresh water**. Even worse, most of that fresh water is trapped in glaciers, ice caps, or buried deep underground, leaving less than 1% easily accessible for human use in lakes, rivers, and soil.\n* **The Water Cycle:** Water on Earth is constantly moving in a closed loop driven by the sun. It evaporates from the oceans, rises into the atmosphere to form clouds, falls back to Earth as rain or snow (precipitation), and flows back into the oceans via rivers and groundwater. \n\n### 3. The Biological Perspective (Life)\n* **The Basis of Life:** Water is the medium in which the metabolic and cellular processes of life take place. Without water, biochemical reactions cannot occur.\n* **Human Body:** The human body is made up of roughly **60% water**. It regulates our internal temperature, lubricates joints, flushes out waste, and protects our organs and tissues. While a human can survive for weeks without food, they can generally only survive **three to four days without water**.\n\n### Summary\nWater is simple in its chemical makeup ($\\text{H}_2\\text{O}$), but profoundly complex in its importance. It shapes the geology of our planet, drives our weather systems, and is the literal lifeblood of every living organism on Earth."
